const FAQ = [
  { q: 'Do I need Cloudflare, or any one vendor, to check signatures?', a: h('No. The key directory sits on the agent\'s domain, and any site or vendor can check it by the draft\'s rules [1]. Each verifier decides which agents it knows. Cloudflare\'s checks the directories registered with it [12].') },
  { q: 'Why not use OAuth instead?', a: h(`Use OAuth for what the agent may do. Web Bot Auth says which website is asking and carries no permissions; an OAuth token carries them [1, 43]. ${a(ID, 'How the two fit together →')}`) },
  { q: 'Does requiring signatures affect search?', a: h('Googlebot doesn\'t sign, and Google tells sites to keep relying on IP addresses, reverse DNS and user agents alongside Web Bot Auth [13, 19]. FORA\'s edge never refuses Googlebot, Bingbot or Applebot [11].') },
  { q: 'Will a signature rule refuse agents I want?', a: h('It can. Google signs only some Google-Agent requests and tells sites to fall back to the older checks [13]. On Arkose\'s network, most legitimate agent sessions carried no verified signature [26].') },
  { q: 'Does Web Bot Auth track my readers?', a: h('No. It identifies agents, and a person reading your site signs nothing. One identifier links an agent\'s requests across sites, so the draft calls it unsuited to an identifier tied to one person [1].') },
  { q: 'Does an agent have to register with each verifier?', a: h('Not by the draft, which asks for no prior relationship [1]. Cloudflare counts signatures from bots that registered their key directory through its bot submission form [12]. DataDome asks operators to apply through a form [29]. AWS WAF checks the signature against the agent\'s key directory and labels the result [27]. A FORA Exchange checks a signature from any key directory that meets FORA\'s rules, and an account takes one signed request [5, 9].') },
];

